# 9. REQUEST VALIDATION

## 9.1 Validation library

Use Zod if it is already part of the approved stack.

Otherwise, inspect the current validation architecture before introducing a new dependency.

## 9.2 Validation requirements

Every endpoint accepting input must validate:

- Required fields.
- Data types.
- String length.
- Numeric ranges.
- Array length.
- Enum values.
- Identifier formats.
- Nested structures.
- Allowed content types.
- Resource relationships.
- Business constraints.

## 9.3 Validation location

Validation must occur on the server.

Client-side validation may improve user experience but must never be treated as authoritative.

## 9.4 Unknown fields

Reject or safely ignore unknown fields according to the endpoint's documented contract.

Do not allow unexpected fields to be passed directly into database update operations.

## 9.5 SQL injection and query safety

- Use parameterized queries.
- Use Supabase query builders appropriately.
- Never concatenate untrusted user input into SQL.
- Validate dynamic sorting fields.
- Restrict filterable fields.
- Avoid unrestricted raw SQL.

## 9.6 Content validation

For user-generated content:

- Validate approved character limits.
- Reject malformed payloads.
- Apply approved moderation checks.
- Sanitize output appropriately.
- Prevent unsafe HTML execution.
- Preserve legitimate user text without unsafe rendering.

The exact maximum lengths, allowed formatting, and content restrictions must come from the Master PRD or approved product decisions.

Do not invent limits. (TBD — owner Dennis, P1 config; see README Open Decisions register, D-22.)

